    {
     "title": "Story 4.5 · 🔑 Three Words and a Number",
     "description": "✨ A long password you can actually remember keeps your accounts safe.\n\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n📖 Story: \"The Password Was 1234\"\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n\n👤 Meet Mr. Sinha\n\n👴 Mr. Sinha is 68 years old and lives in Patna. He has a Gmail, a WhatsApp and a bank app.\n\nFor everything, he uses the same password: 1234.\n*“It is easy to remember,”* he says.\n\nOne day, his grandson Aman, who studies computers, hears this and is shocked.\n“Dadu, that is the first password a stranger would try!”\n\nMr. Sinha is worried now. *“But I cannot remember hard passwords like @#X9q!”*\n\nAman smiles. He shows him a better way.\n\n📱 The idea:\n• Join three or four unrelated words with a number\n• For example: “Mango-River-Lamp-47”\n• It is long, strong and easy to remember\n• Use a different password for every account\n\nMr. Sinha makes his own: “Tulsi-Chai-Cricket-91.”\nHe can picture a cup of tea and a cricket match, and he remembers it easily.\n\n*“I made a strong password, and I did not even have to learn it by heart,”* he says proudly.\n\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n🤔 What If You Use the Same Password Everywhere?\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n\nMrs. Khanna, 63, uses one password for everything.\nIf a thief learns it from one website, they can open her other accounts too.\n\nThat is why every account needs a different password.\n\n💡 Write your passwords on paper and keep it in a safe place at home, not in your wallet or on your phone's wallpaper.\n\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n🛠️ How To Make a Strong Password\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n\n1️⃣ Pick Three or Four Words\n✅ Choose words that have nothing to do with each other, like Tulsi, Chai, Cricket.\n\n2️⃣ Add a Number\n✅ Add a number that is not your birthday, like 91.\n\n3️⃣ Join Them\n✅ Tulsi-Chai-Cricket-91\n\n4️⃣ Make a New One for Each Account\n✅ Use a different password for every account.\n✅ Never share it with anyone who asks.\n\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n🎮 Interactive Activity: \"Mr. Sinha's New Password\"\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n\n🎬 Scenario\nMr. Sinha wants a strong password that he can remember.\n\n❓ Question\nWhat should he do? (Tick all that are correct.)\n\n☐ Join three or four unrelated words with a number\n☐ Make the password long\n☐ Use a different password for every account\n☐ Keep using 1234\n☐ Use his own name and birth year\n\n🔑 Answer\n✅ Join three or four unrelated words with a number\n✅ Make the password long\n✅ Use a different password for every account\n❌ “1234” is the first password a stranger would try\n❌ Your name and birth year are easy to guess\n\n🏆━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━🏆\n🏆 Password Pro Badge Earned!\n🏆━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━🏆\n\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n🌟 Real-Life Benefits\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n\nAfter spending just a few minutes learning this:\n\n✅ Your accounts are harder to break into\n✅ You can still remember your passwords\n✅ One stolen password does not open everything\n✅ You feel safer online\n\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n💭 Reflection Question\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n\nWhich three words, from your own life, could you join with a number to make your next password?\n\n🌟 Digital Saathi · Learn it · Try it · Stay safe"
